WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.580
A changing workload asks a different question from a nameplate calculation.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.200
The power path must support demand over time, including permitted transitions and

3
00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.760
the response of conversion, storage and control.

4
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:14.420
Two workloads with the same average can impose different excursions.

5
00:00:14.901 --> 00:00:18.380
The animation shows demand changing against a constrained delivery path.

6
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:21.560
The curve is illustrative, not measured hardware performance.

7
00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.420
A buffer can supply or absorb energy within its limits, but its

8
00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:28.680
available energy, power and state must support the event.

9
00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.300
It doesn't create unlimited sustained supply.

10
00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:36.380
Next, identify fault domains: parts of the system whose shared dependency can

11
00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:37.040
fail together.

12
00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:41.700
Multiple shelves can still share an input, bus, protection element or control

13
00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:42.280
dependency.

14
00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:46.140
Review which functions remain local and which depend on coordination.

15
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.720
The useful outcome is a topology and response review tied to the

16
00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:54.440
required events, not an assumption that adding shelves or storage automatically removes

17
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.180
every bottleneck.

18
00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:00.220
Specialist energy-storage material is linked for qualification depth beyond the rack boundary.

